  'chromium-path-smoke.js', // THE BROWSER GATE CAN ACTUALLY FIND A BROWSER, and says so loudly when it cannot. The four pilot gates resolved Chromium as `CHROMIUM_PATH || chromium.executablePath()` and launched it -- but executablePath() returns the build playwright-core was PUBLISHED AGAINST whether or not it is installed, and in this container it is not (expects chromium-1228, image ships chromium-1194). Playwright does not refuse a missing executable quickly: the launch HANGS. So the gates emitted no output at all, and the previous handoff recorded "local Chromium remains unavailable under /opt/pw-browsers" as an external proof gap. It was not an absence -- the binary was one directory along the whole time, and all four gates pass against it once found. That is the worst shape a test-infrastructure defect can take: it removes proof while reporting nothing, and the missing proof gets written down as an environment limitation rather than investigated. The other six browser checks had the mirror-image bug, HARD-CODING chromium-1194: correct today, wrong the moment the image updates. Two wrong answers to one question, which is why resolution now has one owner (scripts/lib/chromium-path.js) and E2/E3 assert over the DIRECTORY so a new gate cannot reintroduce either. What is asserted is the resolver's contract rather than "Chromium exists here" (a machine with no browser is a legitimate state): it never returns a path that does not exist, it finds an installed build when playwright-core points at a missing one, PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH replaces the default roots rather than adding to them, an explicit CHROMIUM_PATH wins and FAILS rather than being quietly replaced when wrong, two installed builds prefer the newer, and no browser anywhere throws immediately with a message naming what it looked for instead of handing back something to hang on.
