  'persistence-smoke.js', // THE PERSISTENCE BOUNDARY (in-process, fake DB): a mutation costs what it CHANGED, not the whole platform. Every scheduleSave() used to serialise all 64 stores — every org, user, conversation and inquiry — and ship them to Neon, which sits outside Render, so one conversation turn uploaded the entire product and 4.89 GB of billed egress served 14 MB of actual pages. Pins that the split loses nothing (every persisted key lands in exactly one durable unit, the derived emailIndex in none), that an unchanged save writes NOTHING, that one org changing never rewrites another, that two stores changing commit in ONE transaction, that an emptied unit is DELETED rather than left as a ghost that reappears on the next boot, that a failed write stays dirty and retries (never marking unsaved state saved), that concurrent write transactions never exceed one while a mid-save mutation is still persisted, that split rows reconstruct memory exactly on restart, that session expiry is its own concern and no longer a side effect of saving, and that a group:<nodeId> subject + composite org:user keys + an unrecognised key all partition correctly with no per-store list to maintain.
